Subject: let setgroups follow the kept CAP_SETGID

Redis 7.4+ and Valkey images start the server with
"setpriv --reuid redis --regid redis --clear-groups", which keeps its
capabilities across the UID change (PR_SET_KEEPCAPS, capset) and then calls
setgroups(0, NULL). Under --root-id the real kernel refuses setgroups to the
unprivileged app (EPERM), and fake_id0 only forgave that while the fake
effective UID was 0, so the entrypoint exited 127 with "setpriv: setgroups
failed: Operation not permitted".

fake_id0 already models CAP_SETUID/CAP_SETGID across a UID drop
(caps_active, kept by PR_SET_KEEPCAPS) for setuid and setgid; setgroups now
uses the same model. Nothing is granted for real: group membership is not
emulated, and the kernel credentials stay the app's.

Upstream: termux/proot @ 7266fb3e8516535682f5a9c8f3a7e70f6506eddb (v5.1.107.92), after patches 0001-0010

diff --git a/src/extension/fake_id0/fake_id0.c b/src/extension/fake_id0/fake_id0.c
index e4e55b2..00cb406 100644
--- a/src/extension/fake_id0/fake_id0.c
+++ b/src/extension/fake_id0/fake_id0.c
@@ -1019,12 +1019,17 @@ static int handle_sysexit_end(Tracee *tracee, Config *config)
 		return 0;
 #endif
 
-	case PR_setdomainname:
-	case PR_sethostname:
 #ifndef USERLAND
+	/* setgroups needs CAP_SETGID, which a process keeps across a UID
+	 * drop with PR_SET_KEEPCAPS + capset: setpriv --reuid=... --regid=...
+	 * --clear-groups does exactly that (the Redis and Valkey entrypoints).  */
 	case PR_setgroups:
 	case PR_setgroups32:
+		return handle_perm_err_exit_end(tracee, config, config->caps_active);
 #endif
+
+	case PR_setdomainname:
+	case PR_sethostname:
 	case PR_mknod:
 	case PR_mknodat:
 	case PR_capset:
